{"name":"Ruffled Clam","hex":"#f9f2dc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ruffled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of ruffle"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having ruffles.","Puffed up like a bird's feathers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ruffled"},{"term":"clam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bivalve mollusk of many kinds, especially those that are edible; for example soft-shell clams (Mya arenaria), hard clams (Mercenaria mercenaria), sea clams or hen clams (Spisula solidissima), and other species, possibly originally applied to clams of species Tridacna gigas, a huge East Indian bivalve.","A type of strong pincers or forceps."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dig for clams."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clam"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ruffle (sewing)","blocks":[{"text":"In sewing and dressmaking, a ruffle, frill, or furbelow is a strip of fabric, lace or ribbon tightly gathered or pleated on one edge and applied to a garment, bedding, or other textile as a form of trimming."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Gullager_Salisbury.jpg/330px-Gullager_Salisbury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruffle_(sewing)"},{"title":"Clam","blocks":[{"text":"Clam is a common name for several species of bivalve mollusc. The word is often applied only to those that are deemed edible and live as infauna, spending most of their lives halfway buried in the sand of the sea floor or riverbeds. Clams have two shells of equal size connected by two adductor muscles and have a powerful burrowing foot. They live in both freshwater and marine environments; in salt water they prefer to burrow down into the mud and the turbidity of the water required varies with species and location; the greatest diversity of these is in North America."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clam"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/1280px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg/500px-Singer_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mechanical ruffler by Singer, used on domestic sewing machines","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASinger_Ruffler_Attachment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Flounce_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketch of a flounce","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlounce_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg/500px-Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg/1280px-Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg/500px-Clam_with_its_siphon_out_in_Boothbay_Maine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"